> Nevertheless I was wondering about the following: Would you know of a way I could drop further markers, based on scanning through my markers and omitting as many to reach a desired amount of markers, preferably more or less similarly spaced over the whole genome? Currently I am thinking about running the missing value thresholds again, however at a artificial high level to drop further markers, however that seems to be a bit dodgy and most likely will not results in similar spacing.

There's a function pickMarkerSubset() for choosing a subset of markers with no two markers within some fixed distance of each other.

For example, we might simulate a very-saturated map:

 map <- sim.map(rep(100, 10), n.mar=1001, anchor.tel <http://anchor.tel>=TRUE, include.x=FALSE)

And then simulate a cross with that map:

 x <- sim.cross(map, n.ind=100)
 totmar(x)

If I wanted to thin out the markers so that adjacent markers were always > 1 cM apart, I would do:

  map <- pull.map(x)
  markers2keep <- lapply(map, pickMarkerSubset, min.distance=1)
  xsub <- pull.markers(x, unlist(markers2keep))
  totmar(xsub)

karl

